{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>To enter you into the covenant, etc. and into its oath.</b> The reason for His saying <b>and into its oath</b> and not being satisfied with ״into the covenant״ will be explained. One must further raise the question: why was a covenant necessary at all from the outset, and why was the king's decree, with the fear of the punishments written in the Torah, not sufficient? The explanation of the matter is that a person might reason as follows: let him commit one transgression — namely, to rebel against the fundamental principle — and be punished with a single punishment for one transgression; this would be more advantageous for him than acknowledging HaShem and His Torah, which would subject him to thirty-six karetot, the various death penalties, and other punishments. Therefore HaShem in His wisdom brought them into a covenant to obligate them for each and every detail, and explained the oath along these lines: they swear to fulfill everything, and if they transgress and violate even one of them, the written oath-alah will come upon them. This is what the verse means by saying <b>into the covenant</b>, etc., <b>and into its oath</b>. From now on, even if they contemplate denying the fundamental principle, a single punishment for that sin will not suffice for them — rather, they will be liable for each and every sin according to all the imprecations of the covenant."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:56.013997912Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}